  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 313 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCold air advection persists behind a cold front that will have\nswept in Saturday night. Sunday night's lows slip into the 30s to\nmid 40s. Given the current forecast, Gaines and Dawson counties, \nalong with parts of the Davis Mountains, could see their first \nfreeze of the season by early Monday morning! Subtle ridging aloft\nshall overlay our region Monday, while surface high pressure to \nour north promotes continued cold air advection. Highs Monday \nafternoon are forecast to mainly reach the 50s; although, parts of\nthe northern Permian Basin may struggle to warm beyond the upper \n40s. Monday night's temperatures again fall into the 30s and 40s.\nA reinforcing shot of chilly air arrives Tuesday courtesy of an \nupper level trough swinging from the Rockies into the Great \nPlains. Some CAMs suggest isolated showers may develop near the \nDavis Mountains and parts of SE NM and the Permian Basin early \nTuesday morning, but chances remain low (up to 20%) at the moment.\nHighs in the 50s will then dominate much of the area Tuesday and \nWednesday afternoons, with 60s in portions of our westernmost \ncounties. Return flow sets in Wednesday night, as a surface high \ndevelops to our east. Consequently, temperatures Thanksgiving Day \nand Black Friday trend a couple of degrees warmer than the \nprevious days, although remaining generally in the 50s and 60s. \nLong-range models indicate another storm system arrives at our \ndoor step Friday. There remains uncertainly regarding the exact \nimpacts of this system; however, a signal for increasing rain \nchances is evident in some models. More details to follow as we \nhead into next week!",
